Proof Q&A Benchmark)】\nL50:     * 【40†𝜏³-Banking】\nL51:     * 【41†Terminal-Bench 2.1】\nL52:     * 【42†Terminal-Bench Hard】\nL53:     * 【43†𝜏²-Bench Telecom】\nL54:     * 【44†MATH-500】\nL55:     * 【45†AIME 2025 (American Invitational Mathematics Examination)】\nL56:     * 【46†MMLU-Pro (Multi-Task Language Understanding Benchmark, Pro version)】\nL57:     * 【47†LiveCodeBench】\nL58:   * 【48†Prompt Templates, Answer Extraction and Evaluation】\nL59:     * 【49†Multiple Choice Questions (GPQA, MMLU-Pro)】\nL60:     * 【50†Multiple Choice Extraction Regex】\nL61:     * 【51†Equality Checker LLM】\nL62:     * 【52†HLE (Humanity's Last Exam)】\nL63:     * 【53†HLE Equality Checker Prompt】\nL64:     * 【54†LCR Equality Checker Prompt】\nL65:     * 【55†Mathematical Questions (AIME 2025)】\nL66:     * 【56†Mathematical Equality Checker Prompt】\nL67:     * 【57†Code Generation Tasks】\nL68:       * 【58†SciCode】\nL69:       * 【59†LiveCodeBench】\nL70:       * 【60†Code Extraction Regex】\nL71:   * 【61†Version History】\nL72: # Artificial Analysis Intelligence Benchmarking Methodology\nL73: \nL74: ### Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index v4.3.2\nL75: ## 【2†Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index】\nL76: \nL77: Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index combines a comprehensive suite of evaluation datasets to assess language model capabilities across reasoning, knowledge, maths and programming.\nL78: It is a helpful synthesis of overall language model intelligence and can be used to compare language models. Like all evaluation metrics, it has limitations and may not apply directly to every use case. However, we are confident that it is a more useful synthesis comparison between language models than any other metric in existence today.\nL79: Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index v4.3.2 incorporates 10 evaluations: AA-Briefcase v1.1, GDPval-AA v2.1, AutomationBench-AA, Terminal-Bench 4.0, SciCode, AA-LCR v1.1, AA-Omniscience, Humanity's Last Exam, GDP.pdf, CritPt. Our methodology emphasizes fairness and real-world applicability.\nL80: Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index is a primarily text-based, English-language evaluation suite. We benchmark models for image inputs, speech inputs and multilingual performance separately to the Intelligence Index evaluation suite.\nL81: \nL82: Intelligence Index evaluation suite\nL83: Intelligence Index is calculated as a weighted average across four categories: Agents (30%), Coding (20%), Scientific Reasoning (20%) and General (30%). The weighting emphasizes agentic tasks. Category membership and per-evaluation weights are shown below.\nL84: Category  | Evaluation  | Questions  | Repeats  | Response Type  | Scoring  | Intelligence\nL85: Index Weighting  | Tool\nL86: Usage  | Private\nL87: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL88: Agents (30%)  | AA-Briefcase v1.1  | 91 tasks across 4 scenarios  | 1  | Agentic task completion with file outputs  | Combined Elo from pairwise comparisons on rubric-graded task success, analytical quality and presentation quality  | 15%  | ✓  | [Button: ✓]\nL89: GDPval-AA v2.1  | 220 tasks  | 1  | Agentic task completion with file outputs  | Pairwise comparison (Elo) by judge panel, anchored to DeepSeek V4.1 Flash (max) at 1600, frozen & scaled  | 10%  | ✓  | ✗\nL90: AutomationBench-AA  | 657 tasks  | 1  | SaaS workflow automation with REST API tools  | Objective completion, with zero credit for tasks that trigger a guardrail violation  | 5%  | ✓  | [Button: ✓]\nL91: Coding (20%)  | Terminal-Bench 4.0  | 66  | 3  | Terminal-based task execution  | Test suite pass/fail, pass@1  | 10%  | [Button: ✓]  | ✗\nL92: SciCode  | 288 subproblems (test set)  | 3  | Python Code (must pass all unit tests)  | Code execution, pass@1, sub-problem scoring with scientist-annotated background prompting  | 10%  | ✗  | ✗\nL93: General (30%)  | AA-Omniscience  | 6,000  | 1  | Open Answer  | Accuracy (10%) and 1 - Hallucination Rate (5%) as separate components  | 15%  | ✗  | [Button: ✓]\nL94: GDP.pdf  | 100 tasks across 10 domains  | 5  | Free-form answer grounded in a long PDF  | Rubric-based LLM grading, All-pass  | 10%  | ✗  | ✗\nL95: AA-LCR v1.1  | 100  | 3  | Open Answer  | Equality Checker LLM, pass@1  | 5%  | ✗  | ✗\nL96: Scientific Reasoning (20%)  | HLE (Humanity's Last Exam)  | 2,158  | 1  | Open Answer  | Equality Checker LLM, pass@1  | 10%  | ✗  | ✗\nL97: CritPt  | 70  | 5  | Python Functions, Symbolic Expressions, Numerical Answers  | Official grading server, pass@1  | 10%  | ✗  | [Button: ✓]\nL98: ## 【3†Additional Evaluations】\nL99: \nL100: Beyond the Intelligence Index suite, we run a range of additional evaluations covering multilingual, visual, mathematical and other capabilities. These are reported separately and are not included in the Intelligence Index score.\nL101: \nL102: Artificial Analysis Multilingual Index: Represents the multilingual ability of models. This is based on the Global-MMLU-Lite evaluation across supported languages. We support the following languages:\nL103:   * 🇬🇧 English\nL104:   * 🇨🇳 Chinese\nL105:   * 🇮🇳 Hindi\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGemini 4 Argon Model evaluation (https://deepmind.google/models/evals-methodology/gemini-4-argon)\nciteturn11view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 5; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://deepmind.google/models/evals-methodology/gemini-4-argon\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 90\nL0@P0: Gemini 4 Argon\nL1@P0: Model evaluation\nL2@P0-1: Approach, methodology & resultsApproach\nL3@P1: We evaluated Gemini 4 Argon across a range of benchmarks, including agentic coding, ML\nL4@P1: engineering, computer use, knowledge work, science and math, multimodal capabilities,\nL5@P1: long-context and cybersecurity.\nL6@P1: Methodology\nL7@P1: All Gemini 4 Argon scores are pass @1 except where otherwise noted. \"Single attempt\" settings\nL8@P1: allow no majority voting or parallel test-time compute. All of the results are run with the Gemini\nL9@P1: API with the highest thinking settings unless indicated otherwise below. To reduce variance, we\nL10@P1: average over multiple trials for smaller benchmarks.\nL11@P1: All the results for non-Gemini models are sourced from providers' self reported numbers\nL12@P1: unless otherwise mentioned below. For GPT-6 Astra, Fable 5.1 and Opus 5.5, we default to\nL13@P1: reporting maximum thinking/reasoning settings available, but when reported results are not\nL14@P1: available we use best available reasoning results.\nL15@P1: Additional Details\nL16@P1: Our benchmarks span several capabilities as of September, 2026:\nL17@P1: ● Knowledge work:\nL18@P1: ○ Vals Index results are sourced from Vals AI.\nL19@P1: ○ AutomationBench results use the private set and are sourced from the official\nL20@P1: Zapier public leaderboard.\nL21@P1: ○ Vals Finance Agent v2 results are sourced from Vals AI.\nL22@P1: ○ Harvey’s Legal Agent Benchmark results are sourced from Vals AI.\nL23@P1: ● Coding\nL24@P1: ○ DeepSWE v1.1 results for Gemini 4 Argon are self computed, using a mini-swe\nL25@P1: agent harness. GPT-6 Astra results are reported from the official public\nL26@P1: leaderboard, Fable 5.1 and Opus 5.5 results are taken from their respective\nL27@P1: system cards. We use the highest scoring thinking level for each model as\nL28@P1: reported by Datacurve on the main leaderboard.\nL29@P1: ○ FrontierSWE results are reported from Proximal's official public leaderboard.\nL30@P1: ○ Vibe Code Bench results are reported from the official Vals AI public\nL31@P1: leaderboard.\nL32@P1: ○ Terminal-Bench 4.0 results for Gemini 4 Argon are self computed, all other\nL33@P1: models are reported from the official public leaderboard. We use the highest\nL34@P1-2: scoring thinking level for each model as reported by the Terminal Bench authors.● ML Engineering:\nL35@P2: ○ PostTrainBench v1.1 results for all models are self-computed. We use v1.1 with the\nL36@P2: OpenCode harness for all models and a 10-hour budget on a single NVIDIA H100\nL37@P2: GPU. Performance is measured by a weighted aggregate across four base\nL38@P2: models and seven benchmarks.\nL39@P2: ● Science and math\nL40@P2: ○ Terminal-Bench Science 0.1 results for Gemini 4 Argon are self computed, with\nL41@P2: 6x verifier timeout to address timeout issues with verification. All other results\nL42@P2: are reported from the official public leaderboard.\nL43@P2: ○ RiemannBench results are reported from the official Surge public leaderboard.\nL44@P2: ○ LABBench2 results for all models are self computed. Models are given access to\nL45@P2: a Linux terminal with pre-installed bioinfo tools, Python, and R, as well as internet\nL46@P2: access.\nL47@P2: ● Long Context:\nL48@P2: ○ Graphwalks results for all models are self-computed. The “Up to 128k” subset\nL49@P2: uses the 650 items with context length up to 128k tokens. The “256 to 1M”\nL50@P2: subset uses the 200 GraphWalks problems with context lengths between 256k\nL51@P2: and 1M tokens.\nL52@P2: ● Computer use:\nL53@P2: ○ Agent’s Last Exam results for Gemini 4 Argon are self computed using the\nL54@P2: default ALE-Claw harness and a 5-hour window, with safety filters enabled.\nL55@P2: Responses flagged by safety filters are returned as empty strings, though the\nL56@P2: episode can continue. GPT-6 Astra and Opus 5.5 results are taken from the\nL57@P2: official public leaderboard. Fable 5.1 results are missing from the public\nL58@P2: leaderboard so are not included. We report the binary pass rate.\nL59@P2: ○ OSWorld 2.0 score for Gemini 4 Argon is self computed, maxed over 3 runs with\nL60@P2: a single attempt per run. Responses flagged by safety filters are returned as\nL61@P2: empty strings, though the episode can continue. We report the partial score\nL62@P2: from the offline subset, which has more robust verifiers. We use the github\nL63@P2: OSWorld 2.0 docker, official evaluator, default 1080p resolution, and max step\nL64@P2: length of 500. We use the Gemini CUA harness in the OSWorld 2.0 repo, with\nL65@P2: parallel batch tool calling enabled. We also include a compaction procedure\nL66@P2: following the same setup as reported in the Opus 5.5 system card. We use\nL67@P2: pyautogui for actuation with screenshot-only observation space, and for tools\nL68@P2: we use UI-specific function declarations. Runs were completed with the official\nL69@P2: OSWorld 2.0 team’s 08.08 patch. GPT Astra results were taken from the official\nL70@P2: blog post. Anthropic only reports aggregated results for the online and offline\nL71@P2: subsets combined, so results from Anthropic are not included.\nL72@P2-3: ● Multimodal:○ Chartography results are without tools and taken from the official Surge public\nL73@P3: leaderboard.\nL74@P3: ○ LVBench results are self computed without tools. We use 1FPS for Gemini, 800\nL75@P3: frames for GPT-6 Astra and 300 frames for fable 5.1 and 600 for opus 5.5 due to\nL76@P3: API limitations.\nL77@P3: ● Cybersecurity:\nL78@P3: ○ CWE-bench v1 results are taken from the official public leaderboard. Scores are\nL79@P3: ranked based on pass@1 and ties broken based on pass@4.\nL80@P3: ○ Real world vulnerability is measured using an internal dataset that tests recall\nL81@P3: over recent, confirmed historical vulnerabilities across a diverse set of\nL82@P3: programming languages in popular open source projects. Our models ran in an\nL83@P3: internal version of the Antigravity harness that is not cyber specialized and are\nL84@P3: given access to the source code.\nL85@P3: ○ Wiz Penetration Testing Benchmark is an internal cybersecurity benchmark\nL86@P3: measuring ability to write exploits against real web application vulnerabilities,\nL87@P3: without access to the codebase.\nL88@P3-4: ○ Gray Swan IPI Benchmark results were sourced from Gray Swan.Results\nL89@P4: Gemini 4 Argon results as of October, 2026 are below:\n"
